      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Forgive me, I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the math, shall we?\n\nWhen you make a payment on a student loan, the interest is typically calculated using a formula that takes into account the outstanding balance, the interest rate, and the payment amount. The interest is usually calculated daily or monthly, and then added to the balance.\n\nNow, when you make a payment, the lender will typically apply the payment to the interest first, and then to the principal. This is known as the \"interest-first\" or \"interest-only\" payment method.\n\nFor example, let's say you have a $10,000 loan with a 6% interest rate and a monthly payment of $200. The interest for the month might be $60 (6% of $10,000), and the principal payment would be $140 ($200 - $60). In this case, the interest is eating up a significant portion of your payment, leaving only $140 to go towards the principal.\n\nHowever, if you were to make a payment of $250, the lender would apply the extra $50 to the principal, reducing the balance by $50. This is where paying extra towards the principal can make a",
